+# Desktop archive transport candidate
+
+This candidate extends the patched CtxHop source with three JSON-only commands.
+It treats archive bytes as opaque and does not inspect, extract, or install them.
+The existing configured domain and device authorization apply; no new domain or
+encryption implementation is introduced.
+
+```text
+ctxhop bundle put --input FILE --metadata FILE --json
+ctxhop bundle list --json
+ctxhop bundle get --id DEVICE/SNAPSHOT --output NEWFILE --json
+```
+
+Put/get return `{ "id": "DEVICE/SNAPSHOT", "sha256": "...", "bytes": 123 }`.
+List returns `{ "bundles": [{ "id": "...", "sha256": "...", "bytes": 123,
+"metadata": { ... } }] }`. A legacy passphrase-only domain requests its password
+on stdin for list/get, with prompts sent to stderr. Managed domains use the local
+device grant and the existing generation/membership checks. Disabled devices
+cannot transfer; push-only devices cannot list/get.
+
+Metadata JSON must contain exactly `sessionId` (UUID), `title` (string),
+`sourceCwd` (string), `updatedAt` (RFC3339/RFC3339Nano string), `historyMode`
+(nonempty native backend string), `cliVersion` (nonempty string), `recordCount`
+(nonnegative integer). Unknown, duplicate, differently cased, missing and null
+fields are rejected. Metadata input is capped at 64 KiB; titles at 4096 bytes,
+source paths at 32768 bytes, history mode at 128 bytes, CLI version at 256 bytes,
+record count at one billion. Paths/titles never enter remote object names or
+diagnostics. Plaintext metadata is intentionally returned to the JSON caller.
+
+The namespace is `v1/desktop-bundles/<device>/<32 lowercase hex random snapshot>/`.
+Archive chunks have fixed names `chunk-000` through `chunk-127`, each at most
+8 MiB. Archives are capped at 1 GiB. Each chunk uses existing `crypto.Encrypt`
+bound to its exact object path. The encrypted `metadata` manifest is published
+last and contains the whitelist, whole SHA-256, length, and contiguous chunk
+lengths/digests. A failed upload can leave only its own unlisted encrypted chunks;
+the command does not perform broad cleanup or overwrite snapshots.
+
+Directory-backed objects use `os.Root` to confine file I/O. Symlinks, Windows
+junctions/reparse ancestors, alternate data streams and unsafe IDs are refused.
+Windows publication uses a native same-directory rename through existing
+`x/sys/windows` APIs, with replacement disabled and source/destination parent
+handles. Non-Windows directory publication requires filesystem hard-link support.
+An unsupported publication operation fails closed. The S3 backend retains its
+existing remote interface, with an existence check and random device-owned keys.
+
+Get reads only the bounded manifest and its validated contiguous chunk list.
+It verifies decryption authentication, each chunk length/digest and whole digest
+before creating the final destination using `O_EXCL`. Validation failure removes
+the private verification file and leaves no output. An existing output is never
+overwritten. A disk write failure after successful validation removes the newly
+created output. During that final copy, the new output may briefly be visible;
+callers must wait for successful command completion before importing it.
+
+All verification uses isolated fixtures below `D:\Go\temp`, not installed user
+stores or the shared domain. See `verification-results/bundle-targeted.txt`,
+`bundle-all-suites.txt`, `bundle-relevant-suites.txt`, `bundle-race.txt`, and
+`bundle-vet.txt`. The full `go test ./... -count=1` and related `go vet` passed.
+Race instrumentation could not run: enabling cgo reports that `gcc` is missing
+from PATH. The ordinary concurrent immutable snapshot test passed; it does not
+substitute for a race-detector run. Live cloud
+eventual consistency, remote virtual-filesystem support, full 1-GiB throughput,
+and real domain rotation are not claimed by those local tests.
